  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "The Supreme Court of the United States consists of nine justices—one Chief Justice and eight Associate Justices—who are nominated by the President, confirmed by the Senate, and hold lifetime tenure. The current Court, led by Chief Justice John Roberts, holds a conservative majority, with Justices Thomas, Alito, Gorsuch, Kavanaugh, and Barrett frequently aligned, while Justices Sotomayor, Kagan, and Jackson generally align on the liberal side. Current Supreme Court Justices (as of April 2026): John G. Roberts, Jr. (Chief Justice): Nominated by George W. Bush; took seat in 2005. Clarence Thomas: Nominated by George H.W. Bush; took seat in 1991. Samuel A. Alito, Jr.: Nominated by George W. Bush; took seat in 2006. Sonia Sotomayor: Nominated by Barack Obama; took seat in 2009. Elena Kagan: Nominated by Barack Obama; took seat in 2010. Neil M. Gorsuch: Nominated by Donald Trump; took seat in 2017. Brett M. Kavanaugh: Nominated by Donald Trump; took seat in 2018. Amy Coney Barrett: Nominated by Donald Trump; took seat in 2020. Ketanji Brown Jackson: Nominated by Joe Biden; took seat in 2022. Key Details: Longest Serving: Clarence Thomas (since 1991). Most Recent Appointment: Ketanji Brown Jackson (2022). Education: All current justices attended law school, with several graduating from Harvard or Yale. Roles: The Supreme Court is the final arbiter in federal legal cases and is responsible for interpreting the Constitution. The justices are often, though not always, aligned along ideological lines, with conservative-leaning members appointed by Republican presidents and liberal-leaning members appointed by Democratic presidents.",
